/* app.tcss — Textual CSS for Nightguard Control.
 *
 * TWO RULES, and a reader should not have to leave this header to learn them.
 *
 * 1. NO FIXED HEX. Colour is expressed only through $-variable role names.
 *    The PALETTE roles ($background, $foreground, $error, $success, $warning)
 *    come from the live omarchy colors.toml via theme.py -> register_theme.
 *    The STRUCTURAL roles — the whole $ng-* namespace: fills, outline colours,
 *    outline alphas, the outline-style family, the popup and tooltip surfaces —
 *    come from the live shell.toml via theme.py -> style_variables ->
 *    NightguardApp.get_css_variables(). That second half is why a desktop theme
 *    change moves shapes here and not only colours. The brand is the desktop
 *    theme (D-04, omarchy-native), and tests/test_no_fixed_hex.py scans this
 *    file to keep it that way.
 *
 *    Fixed-alpha ink channels need no variable and are written inline:
 *      $foreground 70%   caption channel — section labels, secondary values
 *      $foreground 50%   quiet channel   — right-hand detail, the hour axis
 *      $foreground 12%   keylines
 *    Textual's own HelpPanel stylesheet does exactly this. What does NOT work is
 *    a $-variable whose VALUE is itself a $-reference (substitution is not
 *    recursive), which is why style_variables() never emits one.
 *
 * 2. THE ACCENT RESERVE IS CLOSED — exactly two places, and nowhere else:
 *      a) one dot beside the wordmark;
 *      b) the verdict word and its countdown, when the verdict is GRACE.
 *    Not the frame. Not a keyline. Not an outline. Not a chip — including the
 *    one filled chip, which inverts to $background-on-$foreground. Not a focus
 *    ring: the cursor rung is fed by the theme's own hover-cursor token, and a
 *    theme that points that token at its accent is making its own decision
 *    through the kit rather than breaching this list.
 *    $primary / $accent / $secondary paint TEXT only, and only in the two places
 *    named above. No outline in this file carries one of the three.
 *
 * Shapes come from $ng-border-style — the glyph family the compositor's live
 * decoration:rounding selects — and from the hairline families hkey (top/bottom)
 * and vkey (sides, and a control row's left spine). Two families are banned
 * outright: `round`, because rounding is 0 and a rounded box plus a hairline
 * frame is the hybrid the reference calls a mistake; and `panel`, because six
 * filled strips on one screen is the version the sketches rejected.
 */

Screen {
    background: $background;
    color: $foreground;
    overflow-x: hidden;                  /* horizontal scrolling never appears */
}

/* --- the device frame (UI-SPEC 7.1) ---------------------------------------
 * Four per-edge hairlines and a 2-column inner gutter: 1 row top, 1 row bottom,
 * 1 column each side. That is the WHOLE frame, and it is why nothing else on
 * the screen needs a box. No region draws its own outline — regions are
 * separated by keylines and by one blank row.
 */
#frame {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1fr;
    border-top: hkey $ng-border-normal $ng-border-normal-a;
    border-bottom: hkey $ng-border-normal $ng-border-normal-a;
    border-left: vkey $ng-border-normal $ng-border-normal-a;
    border-right: vkey $ng-border-normal $ng-border-normal-a;
    padding: 0 2;
    overflow-x: hidden;
}

/* Degradation below 135x46 (UI-SPEC 7.4). Textual has no media queries, so an
 * on_resize handler toggles these classes on #frame; that handler lands with the
 * composition. The drop ORDER is the contract — advisory, then the readout row,
 * then the hour axis, and only then the blanks and the integrity line. The hero
 * is never dropped: it is the screen's one idea. `-tiny` assumes `-compact` is
 * set too (the handler sets both), so it only adds.
 */
#frame.-compact .advisory  { display: none; }
#frame.-compact #readout   { display: none; }
#frame.-compact #hour-axis { display: none; }
#frame.-tiny .blank        { display: none; }
#frame.-tiny #integrity    { display: none; }
#frame.-narrow #band       { layout: vertical; }

/* --- the paint ladder (UI-SPEC 8.3) ---------------------------------------
 * DECLARATION ORDER IS THE MECHANISM. `:focus` counts as a class for
 * specificity, so `.ctl:focus` and `.ctl.-pressed` TIE and the LATER rule wins.
 * Verified: with `-pressed` declared after `:focus`, a focused-and-pressed row
 * painted the pressed colour. The order below — idle, selected, cursor, pressed,
 * staged, refused — is therefore load-bearing and must not be re-sorted.
 *
 * The cursor rung serves pointer AND keyboard, because in this TUI the cursor IS
 * focus. That is also what the theme intends: shell.toml's own comment on the
 * focus-* tokens says to mirror the hover-cursor values so mouse hover, keyboard
 * cursor and tab focus read as one state.
 *
 * NO REFLOW (UIX-05, D-05). Every rung reserves the same 1-column left spine —
 * `border-left: vkey`, in EVERY state including idle, never `none` — and every
 * row is `height: 1` with a 2-cell left inset. Only the colour changes, so no
 * state change can relayout a neighbour.
 *
 * BORDER COLOUR CANNOT BE ANIMATED. Textual's ANIMATABLE set contains
 * `background` and `color` but not `border`, so a state change cross-fades its
 * fill over 120ms and SNAPS its spine colour. That is not a bug and it is not
 * fixable from here — do not spend a day on it.
 */
.ctl {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1;
    padding: 0 0 0 2;
    border-left: vkey $ng-border-normal $ng-border-normal-a;
    background: $ng-color-normal $ng-fill-normal;
    color: $foreground;
    overflow-x: hidden;
    transition: background 120ms linear, color 120ms linear;
    /* Progressive enhancement only: this emits the Kitty pointer-shape protocol,
     * which foot documents, but the terminal that launches ngtui is resolved by
     * xdg-terminal-exec and is not pinned. Nothing depends on it and no test
     * asserts it (OD-3/OD-4). */
    pointer: pointer;
}

/* selected: the theme sets selected-border-width to 0, i.e. "fill only". The
 * column is still RESERVED (D-05 forbids removing it), so the spine is painted
 * in the row's own fill and reads as absent rather than as a louder border than
 * the cursor's. */
.ctl.-selected { border-left: vkey $ng-color-selected $ng-fill-selected; background: $ng-color-selected $ng-fill-selected; }
.ctl:focus     { border-left: vkey $ng-border-cursor $ng-border-cursor-a; background: $ng-color-cursor $ng-fill-cursor; }
.ctl.-pressed  { border-left: vkey $ng-border-cursor $ng-border-cursor-a; background: $ng-color-pressed $ng-fill-pressed; }
/* staged (this product's own rung): the fill stays whatever rung the row is on;
 * only the spine changes, so "staged" composes with cursor/pressed. */
.ctl.-staged   { border-left: vkey $warning; }
/* refused: fill and spine both inherit — the refusal is carried by the words. */
.ctl.-refused .row-value  { color: $foreground 50%; }
.ctl.-refused .row-detail { color: $error; }

/* The three cells of a control row. The label width is a fixed layout constant,
 * not a spacing token: a grid aligns by column count (UI-SPEC 4, exceptions). */
.row-label  { width: 18; text-style: bold; color: $foreground; }
.row-value  { width: 1fr; color: $foreground; }
/* `width: auto` is load-bearing, not tidiness. Measured on textual 8.2.7: a cell
 * with NO declared width in a horizontal row resolves to 1fr against the row's FULL
 * width rather than the remainder, so the detail cell took all 132 columns and
 * starved the 1fr value cell down to ONE — which then wrapped its text to 10 lines
 * inside a height:1 row. The row still reported content_size (132,1) throughout, so
 * the no-reflow control could not have caught it. */
.row-detail { width: auto; color: $foreground 50%; content-align: right middle; }

/* Role classes on the two variable cells. ControlRow carries colour as a ROLE
 * NAME and never as a value (D-12), so these are where the names land. Without
 * them `no site is blocked` and `discord matches nothing` would carry the alarm
 * role and paint nothing — a silent alarm, which is worse than no alarm.
 * `.ctl.-refused .row-value` is three classes and still wins the tie, so a
 * refused row's value drops to the quiet channel whatever role it carries. */
.row-value.error    { color: $error; }
.row-value.warning  { color: $warning; }
.row-value.success  { color: $success; }
.row-detail.error   { color: $error; }
.row-detail.warning { color: $warning; }

/* Section labels are the caption channel: 70% ink, UPPERCASE with one space of
 * tracking. Tracking is interleaved into the STRING (Textual has no
 * letter-spacing) and is permitted for section labels only. */
.section-label { width: auto; color: $foreground 70%; }

/* --- chips ---------------------------------------------------------------
 * Outline chips, one row tall, spine on both sides. Exactly one chip on the
 * strip is filled, and it inverts to ink-on-ground — NOT to the accent. */
.chip {
    height: 1;
    padding: 0 1;
    border-left: vkey $ng-border-normal $ng-border-normal-a;
    border-right: vkey $ng-border-normal $ng-border-normal-a;
    background: $ng-color-normal $ng-fill-normal;
    color: $foreground;
    transition: background 120ms linear, color 120ms linear;
}
.chip.-primary { background: $foreground; color: $background; }

/* --- the hero (UI-SPEC 12) ------------------------------------------------
 * Greyscale on purpose, and boxless: the frame is the only outline on the
 * screen. Widths are 1fr and are measured at paint time — no literal ramp width
 * lives in this file or in the code. */
#ramp, #permission, #hour-axis, #readout {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1;
    overflow-x: hidden;
}
#hour-axis { color: $foreground 50%; }
#readout   { color: $foreground 70%; }

/* --- keylines -------------------------------------------------------------
 * An unstyled Rule breaks two contracts at once: it ships `margin: 1 0`, so one
 * keyline would cost 3 rows instead of 1 and blow the row budget by exactly 4;
 * and it paints in $secondary, which on the omarchy theme equals the accent —
 * about 258 reserved cells across two keylines. Override BOTH properties. */
Rule {
    margin: 0;
    color: $foreground 12%;
}

/* --- tooltips (UI-SPEC 8.5, 10.5) -----------------------------------------
 * The 400ms delay is NightguardApp.TOOLTIP_DELAY; the surface is the theme's own
 * tooltip mapping. */
Tooltip {
    background: $ng-tip-bg;
    color: $ng-tip-text;
    border: $ng-border-style $ng-tip-border;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* --- not-initialized full-screen state --- */
#not-initialized {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    content-align: center middle;
    text-style: bold;
    color: $foreground;
    padding: 2 1;
}

/* --- the header (UI-SPEC 7.2 row 2, 10.1) ---------------------------------
 * One row: the wordmark and its accent dot left, the meta right, and the alarm
 * tag right of the meta when it applies. Every cell has an explicit width —
 * measured on textual 8.2.7, a cell with none resolves to 1fr against the row's
 * FULL width rather than the remainder and starves its siblings. */
#header      { height: 1; width: 1fr; }
#wordmark    { width: auto; text-style: bold; color: $foreground; }
/* The accent reserve, item (a) of exactly two. The ONLY accent on this surface
 * besides a GRACE verdict. */
#wordmark-dot { width: auto; padding: 0 1; color: $accent; }
#header-meta { width: 1fr; color: $foreground 70%; content-align: right middle; }
/* Reverse video, so the alarm survives a monochrome terminal as an attribute
 * rather than as a colour (UI-SPEC 11). */
#weak-tag    { width: auto; text-style: reverse bold; color: $error; }

/* --- blank rows and section-label rows ------------------------------------
 * A blank row is a real row in the budget, not a margin: margins do not appear
 * in a row count and are exactly how a 46-row budget stops being checkable. */
.blank         { height: 1; width: 1fr; }
.section       { height: 1; width: 1fr; }
.section-right { width: 1fr; content-align: right middle; }

/* --- the band (UI-SPEC 7.2 rows 12-17, 7.3) -------------------------------
 * Six rows, split 62 | 1 keyline | 1fr. `#frame.-narrow #band` above restacks it
 * vertically; that is the only place this row count changes. */
#band      { height: 6; width: 1fr; }
#state-col { width: 62; height: 1fr; }
#week-col  { width: 1fr; height: 1fr; }

/* --- the verdict: glyph + word + colour + caption, on one row -------------- */
.verdict-row { height: 1; }

#status-word {
    width: auto;
    text-style: bold;
    content-align: left middle;
}

/* Status-word colour roles (glyph + word + colour — never colour alone). */
#status-word.error   { color: $error; }
#status-word.success { color: $success; }
#status-word.accent  { color: $accent; }   /* the GRACE word — reserve item (b) */
#status-word.warning { color: $warning; }

#hero-caption {
    width: 1fr;
    padding: 0 0 0 2;
    content-align: left middle;
}

/* The screen's one `display`-mass element, three rows of DIGITS3X3.
 *
 * NO `text-style: bold`. Textual swaps the glyph set on it — DIGITS3X3_BOLD —
 * and the heavier set reads as terminal art rather than as the desktop this is
 * imitating (UI-SPEC 5). The colour follows the VERDICT role; the glyphs carry
 * no accent of their own, and `color: $primary` (which this rule used to set
 * unconditionally) was an accent-reserve breach on every non-GRACE verdict. */
#countdown         { height: 3; width: 1fr; color: $foreground; }
#countdown.error   { color: $error; }
#countdown.success { color: $success; }
#countdown.accent  { color: $accent; }     /* the GRACE countdown — reserve (b) */
#countdown.warning { color: $warning; }

/* --- key/value rows inside the band ---------------------------------------
 * `margin: 1 1 0 1` is gone: spacing is now explicit blank ROWS, which are
 * countable against the budget in a way margins are not. */
.kv-row {
    height: 1;
    width: 1fr;
    margin: 0;
}

.kv-label {
    width: 10;                        /* fixed label column (UI-SPEC label-col) */
    text-style: bold;
    color: $foreground;
}

.kv-value {
    width: 1fr;
    color: $foreground;
}

/* --- regions (integrity, ledger) ------------------------------------------
 * Hairline band, not a box: $ng-border-style is the family the live radius
 * selects, and the ink is the keyline channel. The old rule used
 * $surface-lighten-1, and $surface == $background on 4 of the 28 installed
 * themes — which is the measured reason this file fills with alpha-on-ink
 * instead of with a surface role. */
.panel {
    border: $ng-border-style $foreground 12%;
    padding: 0 1;
    margin: 1 1 0 1;
    height: auto;
}

.panel-title {
    text-style: bold;
    color: $foreground;
}

#ledger {
    height: 1fr;                      /* takes remaining space, scrollable */
}

#ledger.collapsed {
    height: 6;                        /* last-5 collapsed view */
}

/* --- dim / secondary text (hmacs, hints, captions) --- */
.dim {
    color: $foreground 70%;
}

.advisory {
    margin: 1 1 0 1;
    content-align: center middle;
}

.hint {
    content-align: center middle;
}

/* --- EditScreen: editable field list + anti-impulse preview --- */
#edit-fields {
    height: 1fr;
    border: $ng-border-style $foreground 12%;
    padding: 0 1;
    margin: 1 1 0 1;
}

.edit-row {
    height: 1;
}

.edit-label {
    width: 42;                        /* fixed key column for aligned values */
    color: $foreground;
}

/* Focused field: reverse video + accent label (accent reserved-list, focus). */
.edit-label.focused {
    text-style: reverse bold;
    color: $accent;
}

.edit-value {
    width: 1fr;
    color: $foreground;
}

/* inline field editor: a one-line Input mounted in the row (no modal screen).
 *
 * This is the ONE place where Textual focus means something other than the
 * cursor — a text field in edit mode — so it is where the theme's dedicated
 * focus ring belongs, and it is what $ng-*-focus is reserved for. The spine is
 * reserved at rest exactly as a .ctl row's is, so entering edit mode cannot
 * reflow the row. */
#inline-edit {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1;
    border: none;
    border-left: vkey $ng-border-normal $ng-border-normal-a;
    padding: 0;
    background: $ng-color-normal $ng-fill-normal;
    color: $foreground;
}
#inline-edit:focus {
    border-left: vkey $ng-border-focus $ng-border-focus-a;
    background: $ng-color-focus $ng-fill-focus;
}

/* The edit-window state, above the fields. It is the first thing on the screen
   because a gate the user only meets after typing reads as a trap. */
#edit-window-banner {
    margin: 0 1 1 1;
    padding: 0 1;
    height: auto;
    text-style: bold;
}
#edit-window-banner.accent     { color: $accent; }
#edit-window-banner.warning    { color: $warning; background: $warning 10%; }
#edit-window-banner.error      { color: $error; background: $error 10%; }
#edit-window-banner.text-muted { color: $foreground 70%; text-style: none; }

/* The per-field anti-impulse preview line (glyph + word + colour). */
#edit-preview {
    margin: 1 1 0 1;
    text-style: bold;
}
#edit-preview.accent     { color: $accent; }
#edit-preview.warning    { color: $warning; }
#edit-preview.error      { color: $error; }
#edit-preview.text-muted { color: $foreground 70%; }

#edit-status {
    margin: 1 1 0 1;
}
#edit-status.success    { color: $success; }
#edit-status.error      { color: $error; }
#edit-status.warning    { color: $warning; }
#edit-status.text-muted { color: $foreground 70%; }

/* --- commit dialog (confirm gate + in-window password + result) ------------
 * The card is a POPUP, so it takes the theme's popup surface mapping rather than
 * a role of its own. Its outline used to be the reserve's third breach. */
ConfirmScreen {
    align: center middle;            /* centre the dialog, not a tiny corner box */
    background: $background 55%;      /* dim the editor behind it */
}

#confirm-box {
    width: 66;
    max-width: 90%;
    height: auto;
    border: $ng-border-style $ng-popup-border;
    padding: 1 2;
    background: $ng-popup-bg;
    color: $ng-popup-text;
}

#confirm-title {
    text-style: bold;
    color: $accent;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

#confirm-line {
    text-style: bold;
}
#confirm-line.accent  { color: $accent; }
#confirm-line.warning { color: $warning; }
#confirm-line.error   { color: $error; }

#confirm-hint {
    margin-top: 1;
}
#confirm-hint.dim    { color: $foreground 70%; }
#confirm-hint.accent { color: $accent; }
#confirm-hint.error  { color: $error; }

#confirm-result {
    margin-top: 1;
    text-style: bold;
}
#confirm-result.success    { color: $success; }
#confirm-result.error      { color: $error; }
#confirm-result.warning    { color: $warning; }
#confirm-result.text-muted { color: $foreground 70%; }
#confirm-result.dim        { color: $foreground 70%; }
#confirm-result.accent     { color: $accent; }

/* masked password field revealed in the dialog on `y` — the second reserved
 * home of the focus ring, for the same reason as #inline-edit. */
#commit-pass {
    width: 1fr;
    margin-top: 1;
    border: $ng-border-style $ng-popup-border;
    background: $ng-color-normal $ng-fill-normal;
    color: $foreground;
}
#commit-pass:focus {
    border: $ng-border-style $ng-border-focus;
    background: $ng-color-focus $ng-fill-focus;
}

/* Text selection inside both editors — the theme's own selection alpha. */
#inline-edit > .input--selection,
#commit-pass > .input--selection {
    background: $ng-color-selected $ng-fill-selection;
}
